INT. FUNERAL PARLOUR VIEWING ROOM - NIGHT

A narrow room of lilac carpet and low lamps. A closed casket on trestles, a spray of white carnations beside it. The only sound is the refrigeration unit breathing behind the wall.

AUGUSTINE "GUS" MARLOWE-DIAZ, 50s, in a tidy black suit, sits in the front row with his hat on his knee. He has the steady manner of a man long used to waiting rooms. His shoes are polished to a high gloss.

The door opens softly. DETECTIVE CORDELIA VANTERPOOL, 40s, in a damp mackintosh, enters without a sound. She stands at the back and does not sit.

CORDELIA
Mr. Marlowe-Diaz.

GUS
Detective. You came.

CORDELIA
You asked me to.

GUS
I did.

She walks the aisle. Her heels leave faint prints in the nap of the carpet. She stops beside the casket. Rests two fingers on its lid, then removes them.

CORDELIA
You've kept the lamp low.

GUS
She disliked bright rooms.

CORDELIA
So I've heard.

GUS
Tea? The undertaker left a flask. It's still warm.

CORDELIA
No. Thank you.

GUS
It's good tea.

He pours himself one anyway. Hands steady. Offers her the second cup with a small, courteous tilt of the head. She takes it, finally, and holds it without drinking.

CORDELIA
You told the desk sergeant you had something to say about the night she died.

GUS
I did. I do. Only I'd like to say it sitting down, and I'd like you to sit too.

She hesitates, then lowers herself into the chair beside him. The cup rattles faintly on its saucer. He notes it, and says nothing.

GUS (CONT'D)
Forty years, I managed that warehouse. Never late. Never ill.

CORDELIA
So the file says.

GUS
And she managed me. In her way.

CORDELIA
In her way.

GUS
She kept the accounts. She kept the house. She kept a good many other things besides.

Cordelia turns the cup in its saucer. The tea has gone still.

CORDELIA
Mr. Marlowe-Diaz, the coroner's report mentions bruising on the wrists.

GUS
Does it?

CORDELIA
It does.

GUS
How unfortunate.

He sips. Sets the cup down with great care, in exactly the ring it left on the saucer before. The refrigeration unit cycles off. The room is suddenly, ferociously quiet.

CORDELIA
The nephew told us she had been afraid.

GUS
Marcus talks a great deal.

CORDELIA
Of whom was she afraid?

GUS
Of the dark, mostly. The dark and the stairs.

CORDELIA
The stairs.

GUS
Steep. Narrow. I told her a hundred times.

Cordelia sets down her cup. Reaches into her mackintosh. Draws out a brown paper evidence bag, and places it on the empty chair on her other side.

GUS (CONT'D)
(not looking at it)
You'll want to be careful. That's an old house. Things come apart.

CORDELIA
This came apart on a Tuesday.

GUS
Tuesday.

CORDELIA
You told us you were at the lodge until midnight.

GUS
So I was.

CORDELIA
The lodge was closed for repairs. The caretaker has a sign-in book. She signed in alone.

For the first time, Gus turns his head. The courteous mask does not slip. It simply stops being worn.

GUS
Cordelia. May I call you Cordelia?

CORDELIA
No.

GUS
She did not suffer.

CORDELIA
That is not for you to say.

GUS
(softly)
No. I suppose it isn't.

He rises, brushes his trousers, and settles his hat on his head. Holds out both wrists, side by side, palms up, as if waiting for a bracelet or a blessing.

Cordelia takes the evidence bag from the chair. Does not open it. Does not need to.

CORDELIA
Sit down, Mr. Marlowe-Diaz.

GUS
I would rather stand.

CORDELIA
Then stand.

Outside, a car door closes. Footsteps on gravel. The lamp flickers once, and steadies.
